  "content": {
    "title": "Iowa State hires UNLV's T.J. Otzelberger to replace Steve Prohm as Cyclones basketball coach",
    "text": "Updated March 18, 2021, 8:06 p.m. CTT.J. Otzelberger made a return to Iowa State once already that proved, at least in part, to be disappointing.What his second homecoming will prove to be remains to be seen, but he'll do it from a position he's long coveted.As Iowa State's head men's basketball coach.Otzelberger, a former Cyclones assistant who just completed his second season as head coach at UNLV, has been hired to guide the Iowa State program back from one of its worst seasons in history, the university announced Thursday.\"There’s no athletic director in the country that can hire a coach and guarantee that coach is going to win,\" Iowa State athletic director Jamie Pollard said in a video statement produced and released by the school. \"You can guarantee integrity. You can guarantee character. You can guarantee fit by doing due diligence. And in this particular case, TJ worked here for eight years.\"We know about his integrity. We know how he fits our culture. That really gave him a leg up.\"Otzelberger will take over for his former boss, Steve Prohm, who was fired Monday after six seasons with the Cyclones. Prohm led Iowa State to three NCAA tournaments, two Big 12 tournament titles and a Sweet 16, but faltered in his final four seasons, going 18-54 in Big 12 play, including an 0-18 mark this past season.Iowa State finished the year 2-22 overall with a program-record 18-game losing streak. It was the first time the program went winless in conference play since 1936-37.\"Unfortunately we anticipated we could be in a situation where we could have a coaching change,\" Pollard said in his video release. \"So for the last several weeks of the basketball season, our staff spent a considerable amount of time identifying candidates that we felt would be a good fit at Iowa State, that understood what it meant to live in the Midwest and what it meant to recruit in the Midwest . And candidates that we thought financially we could make it work.\"Otzelberger has gone 29-30 overall and 20-16 in the Mountain West in two years at UNLV. Prior to joining the Runnin’ Rebels in 2019, Otzelberger helmed South Dakota State for three seasons. He led the Jackrabbits to two NCAA tournaments while winning the Summit League regular season championship all three seasons during his tenure.\"Coach Otzelberger and his family have strong connections to the university and we are excited to welcome them back home,\" Iowa State president Dr. Wendy Wintersteen said in a statement released by the school. \"It was a pleasure for me to meet with T.J. and get to know him during this process.\"I and all of Cyclone Nation look forward to watching coach Otzelberger and the team achieve great things on and off the court in the seasons to come.\"The 43-year-old coach came to Iowa State as an assistant under Greg McDermott in 2006, and he was elevated to associate head coach when Fred Hoiberg was hired to take over the program in 2010. “It’s somewhat rare to get the chance to return home but Iowa State was my first stop as a Division I college coach and it is my wife’s adopted home,\" Otzelberger said in a statement released by the school, referencing his wife, Alison, an Australia native who was a star on the Cyclones' women's team. \"The resources are here to build and sustain a program that competes at a high level in the Big 12 and nationally.\"I’m familiar with the foundation of the school and basketball program and am eager and inspired to sell our vision and re-establish a championship culture.”Otzelberger left Iowa State in 2013 for a two-year stint as an assistant at Washington, but was hired back in the spring of 2015 by Hoiberg. Just months later, though, Hoiberg was hired by the Chicago Bulls, and Otzelberger was a leading contender to replace him. He was one of a handful of candidates to be interviewed and was perceived by some to perhaps even be the favorite to land the job at the time.Instead, Iowa State hired Murray State’s Prohm, who retained Otzelberger as an assistant. Pollard, in his taped statement, cited Otzelberger's lack of head coaching experience as a factor in the decision at the time that helped tipped the scales against Otzelberger.The Cyclones, a preseason top-10 team, went to the Sweet 16 in Prohm's first season, and Otzelberger was subsequently hired by South Dakota State that spring.Even after leaving the program in 2016, Otzelberger has maintained a close relationship with Pollard, who often attended Jackrabbit games and posted frequently on social media about cheering on Otzelberger’s teams.\"If you combine his integrity, his recruiting experience, his knowledge of our culture and his experience as a head coach,\" Pollard said in his video, \"with the fact that T.J. and Alison want to be at Iowa State, it became really obvious to us that T.J. Otzelberger was the perfect coach and the perfect fit for Iowa State University at this particular time.\"How Iowa State navigates the finances of the coaching change remains to be seen.The athletic department has reported a $25 million shortfall due to the COVID-19 pandemic, and that was before firing Prohm, who is due a $5 million buyout. His 2020-21 salary was $2.4 million.Otzelberger was slated to make $1.3 million next season with UNLV, and is reported to have a buyout of $3 million.\"Making a change in any coach, especially the football and basketball coaches, is a significant financial hit to our department,\" Pollard said in his taped statement Tuesday. \"So it’s going to be critical, whoever we hire understands that we’re going to have to be very creative financially in order to make all of this work.\"",
